// The `prelude.store` sub-module: the project's durable key-value store — named values that
// outlive any single run. Where a handler's `var` is state WITHIN a run and `env` is operator-set
// configuration INTO a run, the store is state BETWEEN runs: any run reads what another wrote, the
// operator browses and edits the same tree in the console, and an AI reads a stored note back into
// its context. Values are ordinary Katari values: a document, a data value, a `file`, a secret
// (`string of private` seals at rest exactly like a secret env entry, and stays redacted at every
// user-facing boundary). Writes are last-write-wins.
//
// KEYS ARE AMBIENT. An operation names a path-like key (`"memos/2026-07"`) and nothing else; WHERE
// the key lands is decided by the environment, not by a value threaded through the code. Three
// concepts cover the whole module:
//
//   - WORKSPACE (`workspace`): the store's working directory AND its serial domain, opened as ONE
//     provider. Under `use store.workspace(path = "core")` every operation's key is prefixed with
//     `core/`, and `exclusive` — the critical section a read-modify-write takes its atomicity from —
//     is served by that same install, one section at a time. Nested workspaces accumulate prefixes
//     and nest domains; the outermost bare perform is project-root access, and an `exclusive` no
//     program handler catches is served at the PROJECT ROOT in one durable, project-wide FIFO across
//     every run. An agent dispatched inside a workspace lives in that directory BY CONSTRUCTION — it
//     holds no value it could widen. A path is `/`-separated `safe_segment`s and a malformed one
//     panics, because that construction is only as sound as the strings a program puts in it.
//
//     THE TWO HALVES ARE ONE WORD ON PURPOSE. A prefix without a lane and a lane without a prefix
//     both have spellings here (a nested workspace at a sub-path is the first; a nested workspace
//     whose path is the lane's own name is the second), and neither has a use that the fused form
//     does not serve — while the split form had a failure mode the fused one cannot have: a facility
//     that opened a prefix and forgot the domain wrote read-modify-writes into the project-wide root
//     FIFO, which is legal, invisible, and wrong.
//   - FACILITY: a workspace-relative toolset — a package (a memory, a task list) whose tools open
//     ONE fixed subdirectory (`use store.workspace(path = "memory")`) around their own operations.
//     The path is the package's constant; WHERE the facility's data lives is the CALLING workspace's
//     decision, which is the whole inversion.
//   - SHARED PLACE (`share` / `shared`): data several workspaces must read never travels by path
//     escape — there is deliberately no `..`. It travels as a REQUEST served ABOVE the workspaces,
//     whose clause opens the shared subtree in its own body; a workspace can never capture what it
//     does not serve, so the proxy IS the semantics of sharing. `shared` is that proxy with the CELL
//     factored out: the task is a closure, so ONE request covers every shared cell.
//
// Each operation is a `request` — part of the run's ENVIRONMENT. A program performs it; a handler in
// scope may catch it (the same mechanism that catches `prelude.throw`) to intervene — a workspace
// prefix, a test stub, a redirect. Unhandled, it escalates like any request to the run's OUTERMOST
// environment, the runtime itself, which MACHINE-ANSWERS it against the project's durable rows at
// the PROJECT ROOT — it is never surfaced as a question to a human operator. The answer is durable,
// so a re-run during recovery observes the same value the first attempt did: the store is
// replay-deterministic.
//
// Storing a `file` JOINS its bytes to the project's FILE LIBRARY: the stored file becomes a project
// file (owned by the api root, listed on the console's Files page) that outlives the run that wrote
// it. Overwriting or deleting an entry NEVER frees a file — the store only forgets the reference; the
// file is removed by an explicit delete through the file API or the Files page, and a stored reference
// to a file that was explicitly deleted reads as `gone` (`prelude.files`' contract for any handle to a
// removed file).

@"A lookup that found a value."
data found(@"The stored value." value: unknown)

@"A lookup that found nothing — distinct from a stored `null`."
data absent(@"The key that had no entry, as the environment resolved it (workspace prefixes applied)." key: string)

// One lookup's outcome. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type lookup = found | absent

@"Read the value at @key@, resolved against the surrounding workspace: `found(value)` or
`absent(key)` — a stored `null` is `found(null)`, so presence and value never blur. Pin an expected
shape with `json.validate[T]` on the found value."
request get(@"The path-like key, relative to the current workspace." key: string) -> lookup

@"Read @key@ as a T, resolved against the surrounding workspace, falling back to @fallback@ when the key
is ABSENT or holds a value that is not one — the TYPED store read, and the only one a program should
normally write. A stored cell is an `unknown` no schema guards (an older run's shape, a hand edit in the
console, a key another facility took over), so every read is a boundary read: `get` folded through
`json.validate[T]`, with the mismatch folded to @fallback@ by `prelude.catch`. T is read off @fallback@
— a `get_or(key = ..., fallback = 0)` reads an integer, a `get_or(key = ..., fallback = my_state(...))`
reads that `data` — so the type is normally inferred rather than written, and the fallback is the same
value the caller would have had to supply in a `match` anyway.

DEGRADING IS THE SEMANTICS HERE, not a shortcut around error handling: a durable cell whose shape no
longer fits is a cell an EARLIER version of the program wrote, and there is nothing a reader can do
about that except start over from a value it does understand. Reach for a bare `get` when a MISSING
entry must be told apart from a WRONG-TYPED one (a first-run branch), or when the mismatch itself must
be reported — `json.validate`'s `validation_error` names the offending path and this agent drops it.

ONE INSTANTIATION TO AVOID: a T that itself admits `null` cannot distinguish a stored `null` (which
validates) from a mismatch (which does not) — both come back as `null`, and so does an absent key. Where
that distinction matters, read the bare `get`."
agent get_or[T](
  @"The path-like key, relative to the current workspace." key: string,
  @"The value when the key is absent or does not fit T; its type IS T." fallback: T,
) -> T with get {
  match (get(key = key)) {
    case found(value => stored) -> {
      // The stored value is checked, not assumed: `catch` folds the `validation_error` back into the
      // answer so the mismatch and the absence take the same arm below.
      agent checked() -> T with prelude.throw[json.validation_error] {
        json.validate[T](value = stored)
      }
      match (prelude.catch(task = checked)) {
        case json.validation_error(message => _) -> fallback
        case validated -> validated
      }
    }
    case absent(key => _) -> fallback
  }
}

@"Write @value@ at @key@, resolved against the surrounding workspace, creating or replacing the
entry (last write wins). Any value fits: a document, a data value, a `file`, a secret (sealed at
rest). A stored `file` joins the project's file library — it becomes a project file (visible on the
Files page) that outlives the writing run. Overwriting the entry does NOT free the file it
replaced; a file is removed only by an explicit delete through the file API or the Files page."
request set(@"The path-like key, relative to the current workspace." key: string, @"The value to store." value: unknown) -> null

@"Delete the entry at @key@, resolved against the surrounding workspace; deleting a missing key is
a no-op. Deleting an entry that held a `file` does NOT free the file — it stays in the project's
file library; remove it with an explicit delete through the file API or the Files page."
request delete(@"The path-like key, relative to the current workspace." key: string) -> null

@"A `list` entry: a key holding a value, directly under the listed place."
data leaf(@"The key, relative to the listed place." key: string)

@"A `list` entry: a path segment with more entries below it — descend by listing @path@ deeper."
data branch(@"The segment name, relative to the listed place." name: string)

// One listing's element. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type entry = leaf | branch

@"List what sits DIRECTLY under @path@ in the current workspace: a `leaf` per value-holding key and
a `branch` per segment with entries below it (sorted; a name that is both leaf and branch yields
both). The default @path@ of \"\" lists the workspace itself; a subdirectory path lists below it —
the path parameter is how a prefix travels for listings, since a listing has no key to carry it."
request list(@"The subdirectory to list, relative to the current workspace; \"\" is the workspace itself." path: string ?= "") -> array[entry]

// ── path segments: the one spelling a name in a key may take ─────────────────────────────────────
//
// A key is path-like and a workspace is a prefix of it, so a NAME that lands in either is structural: a
// worker called "a/b" is two directories, one called ".." climbs out of the workspace its tools were
// meant to be confined to, and one called "" collapses a prefix into a stray separator. The geometry the
// store's whole design rests on — an agent dispatched inside a workspace lives there BY CONSTRUCTION —
// is undone by a single unchecked string, and the strings most likely to reach here are the ones a MODEL
// chose (a worker it named, a metric series it invented).
//
// So the charset lives HERE, once, instead of being re-derived per app. The two halves are deliberately
// different shapes, because they answer to different owners: `safe_segment` is TOTAL and answers `null`
// for the app to turn into a corrective sentence (a model's bad name is an ordinary event, not a bug),
// while `scope` PANICS on a malformed path (a program that opened a workspace on unvalidated input has a
// defect, and a defect is the runtime's channel, not a value to route).

@"Canonicalise @name@ into a store path SEGMENT, or `null` when it cannot be one: trimmed, lower-cased,
and then required to be non-empty and spelled only in lowercase letters, digits, `-` and `_`. ONE
offending character rejects the whole name — this is not a sanitiser, and deliberately so: silently
stripping a `/` out of \"a/b\" would hand back \"ab\", a DIFFERENT name than the one the caller asked
for, and two distinct requests would quietly land in one place.

The charset is what a KEY can hold without meaning something else. No `/` (that is a new segment), so no
`.` and therefore no `..` (that would climb out of the workspace a facility exists to confine), no
whitespace, and no case distinction — a store key compares byte-wise, so \"Weight\" and \"weight\" would
be two series a reader sees as one.

Reach for it wherever a name a MODEL or a USER supplied becomes part of a key (a worker's directory, a
metric series, a topic): the answer is the name to store under, and `null` is the app's cue to write its
own corrective sentence back to whoever proposed it. The trimming and lower-casing are part of the
answer, not a precondition — pass the raw text and store what comes back, so the same name typed twice
with different spacing or case is the same cell."
agent safe_segment(@"The proposed name, exactly as it was supplied — untrimmed and in any case." name: string) -> string | null {
  let normalized = string.to_lower(value = string.trim(value = name))
  // Katari has no character classes, so membership is a `contains` over the charset spelled out, once per
  // code point. The loop short-circuits nothing (a `for` visits every element) — the strings are
  // name-length, so the whole check is cheaper than the branch that would.
  let legal = for (let character in string.split(value = normalized, separator = ""), var every: boolean = true) {
    next with { every = every && string.contains(value = "abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789-_", search = character) }
  } then (_characters) { every }
  if (normalized == "" || !legal) {
    null
  } else {
    normalized
  }
}

// Whether a whole workspace PATH is well formed: one or more `/`-separated segments, each of them
// already canonical (`safe_segment` answers the segment itself). Comparing against `safe_segment`'s
// answer rather than re-checking the charset is what keeps ONE definition of a legal name: a path is
// legal exactly when every piece of it is a name `safe_segment` would have handed back unchanged.
agent is_safe_path(@"The candidate workspace path." path: string) -> boolean {
  for (let segment in string.split(value = path, separator = "/"), var every: boolean = true) {
    next with { every = every && safe_segment(name = segment) == segment }
  } then (_segments) { every }
}

// @path@ back, or a PANIC when it is not a well-formed workspace path. A malformed path reaching `scope`
// is a DEFECT and not an outcome: the path a workspace opens is the program's own geometry, so a `..` or
// an empty segment in it means the program routed unvalidated input (a model's word) into its store
// layout without asking `safe_segment` first. Recovering from that is not a thing a caller can do —
// there is no correct directory to fall back to — and returning a value would let the run keep writing
// into whatever place the bad prefix produced, which is the one failure the geometry exists to prevent.
//
// A program cannot RAISE a panic (see the `prelude` header: `panic` is the runtime's own channel and has
// no declaration a program can perform), so the defect is spelled with the one total-to-partial door the
// language has — a zero divisor, which the runtime reports as a panic ("division by zero"). The message
// is therefore the runtime's own and says nothing about paths; this agent's NAME is what identifies the
// defect in the trace, and it exists for that reason as much as for the branch.
agent panic_on_unsafe_path(@"The candidate workspace path." path: string) -> string {
  if (is_safe_path(path = path)) {
    path
  } else {
    let _defect = 1 / 0
    path
  }
}

// ── workspace: the working directory AND its serial domain, in one install ───────────────────────
//
// Writes are last-write-wins, so a read-modify-write is NOT atomic on its own: two concurrently
// dispatched tools (a `parallel for` tool batch) that each read a record, edit it and write it back
// lose one edit. A workspace therefore opens two things at once — the key PREFIX every operation below
// it resolves against, and the serial DOMAIN whose FIFO makes an `exclusive` a critical section.

@"Run @task@ as a CRITICAL SECTION of the nearest enclosing `workspace`: the workspace's SEQUENTIAL
handler calls it in its own body, so two exclusives of one domain can never interleave — the
handler's FIFO is the atomicity, even against a `parallel for` tool batch. The row is FIXED to the
store operations: a critical section reads, computes and writes, NOTHING ELSE — any other input is
computed BEFORE entering and closed over — so a section can never block on a model, a network, or
another domain. TWO GEOMETRY FACTS, both load-bearing: the task runs AT THE WORKSPACE INSTALL SITE,
not at this perform site — so a facility's subdirectory prefix must be applied INSIDE the task body
(`use store.workspace(path = \"memory\")` as the task's first move; a workspace around the perform
site does not travel with the task, but the install site's own enclosing workspaces do apply). And an
`exclusive` binds to the NEAREST enclosing workspace: an inner domain shadows an outer one BY DESIGN
— the nearest domain is the semantics, exactly as the nearest prefix is. The task's answer crosses
the handler as `unknown`; narrow it back with a `match` (or `json.validate[T]`). With NO workspace
installed above the perform, the RUNTIME serves it as the outermost domain — the task runs at the
PROJECT ROOT (project-root keys) in one durable, project-wide FIFO across every run."
request exclusive(
  @"The critical section; store operations only — compute everything else before entering and close over it." task: agent (value: null) -> unknown with get | set | delete | list,
) -> unknown

@"Open the WORKSPACE at @path@ under the current one, for the extent of @continuation@ — the
`use store.workspace(path = \"core\")` form, and the store's ONE provider. It installs both halves of
a working directory:

  - THE PREFIX. Four parallel handlers catch the store operations and RE-PERFORM them outward with
    the prefixed key (`get(key = \"index\")` under a workspace at \"core\" escalates as
    `get(key = \"core/index\")`; a `list` gets @path@ prepended to its own path parameter), so nested
    workspaces accumulate prefixes and the outermost bare perform is project-root access — unhandled
    at the very top, the runtime machine-answers against the project's durable rows. Those four are
    PARALLEL on purpose: prefixing is stateless arithmetic and must not serialize the traffic passing
    through it (a turn's tool batch stays concurrent).
  - THE DOMAIN. One sequential handler serves `exclusive` by calling the section in its own body, so
    the FIFO applies critical sections one at a time, in arrival order — which is the whole point (a
    parallel clause would forfeit the atomicity). Sections re-perform their store operations from
    THIS install site, so they land under THIS workspace's prefix.

The install order between them is fixed here and is not a preference: the prefix handlers sit OUTSIDE
the domain, so a critical section's keys resolve through the workspace it was opened in. A nested
workspace is therefore a nested prefix AND a nested domain, exactly as it reads, and an `exclusive`
performed below it binds to the nearest one.

A workspace is a ONE-WAY DESCENT: there is deliberately no `..`, so data several workspaces share is
NOT reached by path escape — it is reached by a REQUEST served ABOVE the workspaces, whose clause
opens the shared subtree in its own body. That proxy is the semantics: a request is what an inner
install can never capture by accident. `share` / `shared` below is that proxy, generalised once so it
need not be respelt per shared cell.

@path@ is one or more `/`-separated SEGMENTS, each spelled as `safe_segment` requires (lowercase
letters, digits, `-`, `_`). A path that is not — an empty segment, a `..`, a stray space, a name a
model chose and nobody checked — PANICS here rather than opening a workspace somewhere else: the
descent is the program's own geometry, so a malformed one is a defect in the program and not an
outcome a caller could route. Validate a name that came from OUTSIDE the program with `safe_segment`
first, and render its `null` into whatever sentence the proposer should read.

ONE IMPRECISION TO KNOW ABOUT, because it shows up in the escalation report. All five clauses are
installed, and each re-performs its own operation, so this agent's row names all four store
operations whatever the continuation actually does — a caller that only reads still carries `set` and
`delete` outward. The row is sound (a workspace really can perform all four on its continuation's
behalf) but it is coarser than the truth, so the report reads as though a read-only agent could
delete. Narrowing it needs the row to follow which requests the continuation performs, which the
language cannot express yet; until it can, read a workspaced agent's store row as \"reaches the
store\" rather than as the set of operations it uses."
agent workspace[R, effect E](
  @"The workspace's path under the current one, e.g. \"core\" or \"workers/scribe\"; `/`-separated `safe_segment`s, and a malformed one panics." path: string,
  @"Runs with every store operation prefixed AND `exclusive` served; its result is the provider's result." continuation: agent (value: null) -> R with {...E, get, set, delete, list, exclusive},
) -> R with E | get | set | delete | list {
  // Clause parameter names are structural, so the `list` clause below MUST bind `path` — which
  // would shadow this agent's own `path`. The prefix is rebound once, before the handlers — and the
  // rebinding is where the descent is checked, so no handler below can ever prefix with a bad one.
  let base = panic_on_unsafe_path(path = path)
  // PARALLEL on purpose: prefixing is stateless arithmetic, and a sequential handler here would
  // needlessly serialize a turn's concurrent tool batch through the workspace.
  use parallel handler {
    request get(key: string) {
      next get(key = f"${base}/${key}")
    }
    request set(key: string, value: unknown) {
      next set(key = f"${base}/${key}", value = value)
    }
    request delete(key: string) {
      next delete(key = f"${base}/${key}")
    }
    request list(path: string) {
      // An empty incoming path lists the workspace itself, so only the prefix travels outward.
      if (path == "") {
        next list(path = base)
      } else {
        next list(path = f"${base}/${path}")
      }
    }
  }
  // Deliberately NOT a `parallel handler`, and deliberately INSIDE the prefix: the FIFO of a
  // sequential handler is what makes an exclusive a critical section (tasks run one at a time, in
  // arrival order), and re-performing from here routes the section's keys through the four clauses
  // above — which is what puts a critical section in the workspace it was opened in.
  use handler {
    request exclusive(task: agent (value: null) -> unknown with get | set | delete | list) {
      next task(value = null)
    }
  }
  continuation(value = null)
}

// ── share: the shared place — ONE request for every cell above the workspaces ─────────────────────
//
// A desk opens its OWN workspace per dispatch, so knowledge several desks must share (a soul layer, a
// digest, a registry) sits ABOVE every one of them and is reachable from none — there is deliberately
// no `..`. The answer has always been a request served above the workspaces whose clause opens the
// shared subtree in its own body. That answer is right and it does not SCALE: a request per CELL means
// a `request` declaration, a handler clause and a re-spelt subdirectory workspace for every cell worth
// sharing, a fixed cost per cell that grows with the app.
//
// `share` is that proxy with the cell factored OUT of it, on `exclusive`'s geometry exactly: the
// detail rides a CLOSURE rather than a name, so ONE request covers every shared cell. `shared(task)`
// sends the task UP to run where `share` was installed — that site's workspaces, that site's serial
// domain — and the task opens whichever subdirectory it wants as its first move. What a per-cell
// request bought is not lost: `shared` is still a request an inner workspace cannot capture, so the
// shared place stays exactly as unreachable-by-accident as it was.

@"Run @task@ AT THE NEAREST ENCLOSING `share`'s install site — that site's workspaces, that site's
serial domain — and hand back what it answered: the ONE request by which a desk reaches knowledge
living ABOVE its own workspace. The geometry is `exclusive`'s, and both halves are load-bearing. The
task is a CLOSURE that travels UP and runs THERE, so a workspace around this PERFORM does not travel
with it (the INSTALL site's own enclosing workspaces do apply) — which means a subdirectory of the
shared place is opened INSIDE the task body (`use store.workspace(path = \"shared/soul\")` as its first
move), and that is exactly what lets one `share` serve every shared cell instead of one named request
per cell. And it binds to the NEAREST enclosing `share`: an inner shared place shadows an outer one BY
DESIGN, the same dynamic-scope rule the nearest workspace follows. The
row is FIXED to the store operations plus `exclusive`: a task reads, computes and writes, and takes
ATOMICITY by wrapping a read-modify-write in `store.exclusive` INSIDE the task — which lands in the
shared place's own serial domain, since that is where the task is running. Nothing else is in the row,
so a shared access can never block on a model, a network or another domain. The answer crosses back as
`unknown`; narrow it with a `match` (or `json.validate[T]`) at the perform site.
Unlike the store's four operations and `exclusive`, this one is NOT runtime-served, deliberately:
there is no project-root shared place, because a shared place is somewhere a program CHOSE and a
forgotten install must never quietly resolve to project-root keys. With no `share` above it a `shared`
rides to the run root as an ordinary unanswered request — the fate of any provider-backed request that
lost its provider — so the real guard is the effect row, which keeps `shared` visible in every
signature between the perform and the install."
request shared(
  @"What to do in the shared place; store operations only — compute everything else before entering and close over it." task: agent (value: null) -> unknown with get | set | delete | list | exclusive,
) -> unknown

@"Open the SHARED PLACE for the extent of @continuation@ — the bare `use store.share` form (no
arguments, no type arguments: the shared place is WHERE it is installed, exactly as a workspace is). Serves `shared` by calling its task in the clause body, so a task runs HERE — under this site's
workspaces, over this site's serial domain — while the desks below go on opening their own workspaces
per dispatch and reaching nothing of each other's. THE INSTALL DISCIPLINE, in this order: the app's
workspace, then `share`, then the desks (`use store.workspace(path = \"assistant\")` then
`use store.share`). The order is a CONTRACT, not a habit: a task's row carries `exclusive` and this
provider re-performs it outward, so `share` must sit INSIDE a workspace for a shared critical section
to land in that workspace's domain — which is what the ordinary install order above already gives. The order is legible in the TYPE, not only in prose: install `share` above the domain
instead and its `exclusive` finds nothing to catch it, so `store.exclusive` surfaces in the enclosing
signature and those sections ride to the runtime's project-wide root FIFO — legal, and visibly a
different program. The handler is PARALLEL on purpose: sending a closure upward is not an ordering
decision, and a sequential clause would queue three desks' concurrent reads of the shared place behind
one another for nothing; ordering is `exclusive`'s job here as everywhere, taken INSIDE the task. A
second `share` below this one captures every `shared` under it — the ordinary dynamic-scope rule, and
the way a deliberately NARROWER shared place (a sub-team's own commons, shared among its desks and
invisible to the app's) is expressed. KNOW WHAT THE GENERALISATION COSTS: a request per cell is also a
CAPABILITY per cell — a desk holding `read_digest` and not `save_digest_entry` cannot write the digest
whatever it does — and one `shared` is one capability over the WHOLE shared place. Where a boundary
BETWEEN shared cells is the semantics, keep the named request for that cell; the two compose, since a
named proxy installed below this one can perform `shared` in its own clause."
agent share[R, effect E](
  @"Runs with `shared` served; its result is the provider's result." continuation: agent (value: null) -> R with {...E, shared},
) -> R with E | get | set | delete | list | exclusive {
  // PARALLEL on purpose, and the opposite call from a workspace's `exclusive` clause: routing a closure
  // to the shared place
  // decides WHERE, never WHEN. A sequential handler here would make the shared place a bottleneck for
  // every desk that merely READS it, while buying no atomicity the task's own `exclusive` does not
  // already buy.
  use parallel handler {
    request shared(task: agent (value: null) -> unknown with get | set | delete | list | exclusive) {
      // `next` re-performs from THIS install site, which is the whole mechanism: the task's store
      // operations and its `exclusive` escalate outward from here, through this site's workspaces and
      // into this site's serial domain — never through the caller's.
      next task(value = null)
    }
  }
  continuation(value = null)
}
